[s0l0!-header][h1]Hello World![/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

As when learning any new language, the place to start is with the classic "Hello World!" program: [code format="c" codeId="1619"]#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
  printf("Hello, World!\n");
  return 0;
}[/code]
Let's break down the code to understand each line:
[b]#include <stdio.h>[/b] The function used for generating output is defined in [b]stdio.h[/b]. In order to use the [b]printf [/b]function, we need to first include the required file, also called a [b]header file[/b].

[b]int main()[/b] The [b]main[/b]() function is the entry point to a program. Curly brackets { } indicate the beginning and end of a function (also called a code block). The statements inside the brackets determine what the function does when executed.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

